<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>If I’m exhausted after conversations, does it mean my hearing aids are set wrong?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. You might have good audibility but high listening effort because of noise, echo, or multitasking. That said, a fine-tune can help. Ask your audiologist to evaluate speech-in-noise performance, try stronger directionality or noise reduction, and consider a remote mic. Track your fatigue on a 0–10 scale in different settings to guide tweaks.</p>

<h3>Can people with normal hearing also have high listening effort?</h3>
<p>Yes. Everyone’s effort rises in loud, echoey, or multi-talker spaces, or when they’re tired or stressed. Some people with normal audiograms still struggle in noise and feel disproportionate fatigue. An audiologist can screen for hidden difficulties in noise and suggest strategies and accessories that improve the signal-to-noise ratio.</p>

<h3>Are noise-cancelling headphones better than hearing aids for reducing effort?</h3>
<p>They solve different problems. Active noise-cancelling (ANC) headphones reduce steady background noise and can lower effort for media or one-on-one calls. Hearing aids amplify speech frequencies, use directional mics, and keep you aware of your surroundings. In very loud places, over-ear ANC plus a remote mic to the talker can be an energy-saving combo, but ask your clinician about safety and situational awareness.</p>

<h3>What single change usually makes the biggest difference?</h3>
<p>Improving the signal-to-noise ratio. A simple remote microphone placed near the talker often beats any setting change. Positioning matters too: move closer, face the talker, and put noise behind you.</p>


